    id: "article-1768749211212",
    title: "How to Choose a Villa in Bali: What to Pay Attention To",
    slug: "how-to-choose-a-villa-in-bali-what-to-pay-attention-to",
    category: "rent",
    content: "<p>Choosing a villa in Bali can be simple and enjoyable if you understand your goals and the local rental logic. This guide focuses on practical decisions and real market behavior, especially for people planning more than a short vacation.</p><hr><h2 class=\"heading\">1. Decide on the Length of Your Stay</h2><p>The first and most important step is to clearly define how long you plan to stay in Bali.</p><p>If you are staying in a <strong>tourist villa</strong>, the maximum comfortable stay is usually up to <strong>two months</strong>. If you are not planning to settle in one place, it often makes more sense to live <strong>one to three weeks in different areas</strong> to better understand the island.</p><p>The most popular areas to explore this way are:</p><ul><li><p>Canggu</p></li><li><p>Uluwatu</p></li><li><p>Nusa Dua</p></li><li><p>Ubud</p></li><li><p>Sanur</p></li><li><p>Amed</p></li></ul><p>For <strong>short-term stays</strong>, the easiest platforms are:</p><ul><li><p>Booking</p></li><li><p>Airbnb</p></li><li><p>Agoda</p></li></ul><p>They offer flexibility, clear rules, and fast booking without deep involvement in the local rental market.</p><hr><h2 class=\"heading\">2. Planning a Long-Term Stay (2 Months or More)</h2><p>If you plan to stay <strong>two months or longer</strong> and want to live in <strong>one specific area</strong>, the strategy changes completely.</p><p>For long-term rentals, the best source is <strong>Facebook</strong>. Use simple search logic by typing the <strong>area name + the word “rent”</strong>. Then join the most active and popular local groups.</p><p>This is where most owners, agents, and local contacts publish long-term villas and houses.</p><hr><h2 class=\"heading\">3. Facebook Marketplace</h2><p>Facebook Marketplace is another useful tool. Sometimes very good options appear there, especially for long-term rentals.</p><p>Results can be unpredictable. You might find a perfect villa in one day, or spend one to two weeks searching.</p><p>If you are looking for a <strong>high-quality villa for one year</strong>, the search period can be even longer. Good long-term villas are limited and often booked quickly.</p><hr><h2 class=\"heading\">4. Average Rental Prices in Bali</h2><p>Below is a <strong>general price overview</strong>. Prices vary depending on the area, quality, privacy, and demand.</p><ul><li><p><strong>5–10 million IDR per month</strong>: A room in a guesthouse or shared villa</p></li><li><p><strong>10–15 million IDR per month</strong>: A private house or villa; closer to 15 million may include a private pool</p></li><li><p><strong>15–25 million IDR per month</strong>: A two-bedroom private villa; quality strongly depends on location, noise level, view, space, interior options, and design</p></li><li><p><strong>25 million IDR and above</strong>: Three-bedroom villas and larger properties</p></li></ul><p>In <strong>Canggu</strong>, prices are generally higher across all categories because it is currently the most popular and expensive area for tourists and short-term residents.</p><hr><h2 class=\"heading\">5. How to Communicate and Negotiate</h2><p>Be respectful in communication. You often cannot know who you are talking to: the owner, a family member, a friend, or an agent.</p><p>Every owner has a different understanding of pricing and preferred tenants. <strong>Do not aggressively negotiate online.</strong> Real negotiation should happen <strong>only in person, during a villa viewing</strong>.</p><p>This shows respect for the price, the owner’s time, and the property itself.</p><p>If you are a polite, clean individual or family, there is a chance you may receive a small discount. However, never expect it. Many owners have strict pricing due to loans, new construction, or long-term financial plans.</p><hr><h2 class=\"heading\">6. Always View the Villa in Person</h2><p>Either inspect the villa yourself or send a trusted friend. In some cases, it is worth paying a third person to inspect the villa for you, record videos, and check details based on your criteria.</p><p><strong>Never pay for a villa without viewing it.</strong> <strong>Never send a large deposit online</strong> (more than 1–2 million IDR).</p><p>The risk of scams is real.</p><p>If you or your trusted contact view the villa, and it truly fits your needs, the price looks reasonable compared to the market, and everything feels transparent, <strong>pay the deposit and secure the villa immediately</strong>.</p><p>Good villas are often booked within one day.</p><hr><h2 class=\"heading\">What’s Next</h2><p>In the next article, I will explain:</p><ul><li><p>how to protect yourself from unpleasant surprises</p></li><li><p>common issues tenants face in Bali villas</p></li><li><p>what to check before signing or paying</p></li></ul><p>Understanding these details will save you money, time, and stress when choosing housing in Bali.</p>",
