    id: "article-1768759096663",
    title: "What to Look for When Choosing Housing in Bali",
    slug: "what-to-look-for-when-choosing-housing-in-bali",
    category: "rent",
    content: "<h2 class=\"heading\">Hidden Pitfalls of Renting Villas and Houses</h2><p>Choosing a villa or house in Bali involves many details that are not obvious at first glance. This article explains the most common hidden pitfalls and how to avoid unpleasant surprises, especially if you are searching for housing while already on the island.</p><h3 class=\"heading\">Table of Contents</h3><ol><li><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer nofollow\" class=\"article-link\" href=\"#construction-noise\">Construction Noise</a></p></li><li><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer nofollow\" class=\"article-link\" href=\"#Roads-and-Daily-Access\">Roads and Daily Access</a></p></li><li><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer nofollow\" class=\"article-link\" href=\"#Distance-to-Supermarkets\">Distance to Supermarkets</a></p></li><li><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer nofollow\" class=\"article-link\" href=\"#Privacy-and-Access-to-the-Villa\">Privacy and Access to the Villa</a></p></li><li><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer nofollow\" class=\"article-link\" href=\"#Humidity,-Mold,-and-Smells\">Humidity, Mold, and Smells</a></p></li><li><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer nofollow\" class=\"article-link\" href=\"#Roofs,-Floors,-and-Rain\">Roofs, Floors, and Rain</a></p></li><li><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer nofollow\" class=\"article-link\" href=\"#Living-With-Children-or-Elderly-People\">Living With Children or Elderly People</a></p></li><li><p>Animals Nearby</p></li><li><p>Light and Windows</p></li><li><p>Kitchen Design</p></li><li><p>Bathrooms and Bathtubs</p></li><li><p>Cleanliness and Maintenance</p></li><li><p>Owners and Communication</p></li><li><p>Payments and Safety</p></li><li><p>What Is Included in the Price</p></li></ol><hr><h2 class=\"heading\">Construction Noise</h2><p>Construction is the number one problem in Bali. It is everywhere.<br>Even if a villa is quiet today, construction may start next door next month. Noise, dust, and early morning work can seriously affect both short stays and long term living.</p><p>Always look around for empty land nearby, ask neighbors, and clarify whether construction is planned. Construction noise is one of the most common reasons tenants decide to move out.</p><hr><h2 class=\"heading\">Roads and Daily Access</h2><p>Using a scooter greatly increases the number of available rental options. However, many villas are located at the end of narrow roads with no lighting and slippery surfaces after rain.</p><p>For inexperienced riders, especially women, this can become stressful. Wet concrete, poor visibility at night, and uneven roads require constant attention.</p><hr><h2 class=\"heading\">Distance to Supermarkets</h2><p>Comfortable daily life in Bali strongly depends on access to supermarkets such as Pepito, Bintang, Delta Dewata, and CocoMart.</p><p>If supermarkets are far away, regular trips can become tiring over time. At the same time, due to dense construction, villas with space, views, and natural light are often located further from the center of each area. This balance between convenience and quality is normal in Bali.</p><hr><h2 class=\"heading\">Privacy and Access to the Villa</h2><p>Many villas are accessed through traditional family compounds. On the way to your home, you may pass relatives, children, dogs, chickens, and roosters every day.</p><p>For some people, this feels safe and welcoming. For others, it reduces privacy. This is not a problem by itself, but it is important to understand your own comfort level before renting.</p><hr><h2 class=\"heading\">Humidity, Mold, and Smells</h2><p>Humidity and mold are one of the most serious long term issues in Bali housing. Unlike construction, mold does not simply disappear.</p><p>Avoid villas located near rivers, cliffs, or under dense trees. Well ventilated and dry locations are always preferable. During viewings, pay attention to smells, ceilings, corners, and wardrobes.</p><hr><h2 class=\"heading\">Roofs, Floors, and Rain</h2><p>Check the condition of the roof and look for any signs of leaks. Also pay attention to floor materials. Many houses use ceramic tiles, which become very slippery during the rainy season, especially in areas you use frequently.</p><hr><h2 class=\"heading\">Living With Children or Elderly People</h2><p>If children or elderly people will live in the villa, carefully assess safety. Pay attention to pool depth, stairs, sharp corners, and overall layout. Not all villas are designed with these needs in mind.</p><hr><h2 class=\"heading\">Animals Nearby</h2><p>Traditional Balinese households often keep animals such as chickens, roosters, dogs, and sometimes pigs. Roosters can be particularly noisy and not only in the morning.</p><p>Always observe neighboring houses to understand the real sound environment around the villa.</p><hr><h2 class=\"heading\">Light and Windows</h2><p>Many houses in Bali are surprisingly dark, with very few windows.<br>Living in a tropical environment without natural light can significantly reduce comfort. Choosing a villa with good daylight and open views greatly improves long term living quality.</p><hr><h2 class=\"heading\">Kitchen Design</h2><p>Kitchens in Bali are often open air, small, and not designed for intensive cooking. Closed kitchens for reasonable prices are rare and sometimes impossible to find.</p><p>Before renting, decide whether an open kitchen suits your lifestyle and how important cooking is for you.</p><hr><h2 class=\"heading\">Bathrooms and Bathtubs</h2><p>Not all villas have bathtubs. Some offer only outdoor or semi open showers. Decide in advance whether a bathtub is important for you or if a shower is sufficient.</p><hr><h2 class=\"heading\">Cleanliness and Maintenance</h2><p>Pay attention to how clean the villa is during the viewing. In most cases, cleaning is done by someone from the owner’s family.</p><p>If the villa already looks poorly maintained, similar issues may continue later, especially with cleaning schedules and general upkeep.</p><hr><h2 class=\"heading\">Owners and Communication</h2><p>Good communication with the owner is essential. Most Balinese owners are kind, friendly, and responsive.</p><p>Still, notice how willing they are to solve small issues such as repainting walls, replacing fixtures, or improving internet quality. This often reflects how comfortable your stay will be.</p><hr><h2 class=\"heading\">Payments and Safety</h2><p>Scams are rare in Bali, but caution is always necessary. Warning signs include rushing, prices far below market level, and unclear explanations about ownership or rental rights.</p><p>Formal contracts are often not used, and many agreements are based on trust. Extra attention is recommended when renting long term, especially for yearly payments.</p><hr><h2 class=\"heading\">What Is Included in the Price</h2><p>Usually included in the rental price are villa cleaning, pool maintenance, bed linen changes once every one or two weeks, and internet access.</p><p>Electricity is almost always paid separately, which is normal in Bali. Costs depend on air conditioner usage and house size. Water and gas are inexpensive and rarely cause issues.</p><hr><p>This guide should help you approach housing selection in Bali calmly and realistically.<br>In the next article, I will explain how to avoid legal and financial issues and what to pay attention to when renting a villa for six months or one year.</p>",
